{"id": "64e1793d-1169-4fb4-b822-8a27f42be318", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:19:41.366241", "source": "user", "parent_id": "b94c3d2a-8184-4a44-b30e-eac1c76a5189", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Inspection is accepted and the overall approach is approved: design-only target, provisioned-environment primary spec with a stdlib-only reduced pilot as fallback, GP-mechanism additive-noise DGP with additive shared-function latents, CAM / FCI baselines, and the latent-free control condition. Please now write the full experiment design. Hold the structure you proposed, but resolve these specific points in the deliverable:\n\n1) Scoring convention must be pinned down, not left as an open mapping choice. Define one primary target object (the observed marginal graph with bidirected marks for pairs sharing a latent, plus its Markov closure) and state exactly how SHD / adjacency P-R / orientation accuracy are computed on it. Give the explicit decision rule for the error taxonomy: what counts as \"reversed edge that should be bidirected\", \"true directed edge reported as bidirected\" (over-invocation of latents), and how unmarked/bidirected-vs-directed mismatches enter SHD. Report orientation recall separately for confounder-free edges.\n\n2) Make stage 2 (confounded vs orientable) operational. Specify the actual statistic(s) you would use and what each is testing: the residual-dependence test (HSIC permutation? additive-GAM residual correlation? at what subsample/alpha), and the non-additivity test of the joint fit. State the decision rule that turns test outcomes into a bidirected mark, and be explicit that \"ambiguous orientation\" alone is not evidence of confounding. Note where you are deviating from the published CAMUV construction because the source is unavailable.\n\n3) Add stage-attributable evaluation so the result is interpretable rather than a single end-to-end number: report (a) skele