stic); \u03bb \u2208 {0, 0.5, 1.0}; n \u2208 {500,1000,2000}; p \u2208 {5,10,20}; function family (GP-RBF vs. random cubic splines vs. small random MLPs).\n\n## 9. Pre-registered threshold-sensitivity analysis (resolves artifact concern)\n\nSweeps (paired over identical datasets and seeds): \u03b1_c \u2208 {0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1} for stage-2 tests; \u03b1_stop \u2208 {0.001, 0.01, 0.05} for CAM/orientation; stage-1 penalty multiplier \u03c1 \u2208 {1,2,3}\u00d7BIC-equivalent. B, m, K fixed except in the explicit K/B sensitivity column.\n\nPre-registered robustness criteria for the claim \"CAMUV's advantage over CAM is not a tuned-threshold artifact\":\n1. Paired \u0394 metrics (SHD, orientation recall on confounder-free edges, E1 rate) between CAMUV and CAM-shared-skel have consistent sign at \u22654 of 5 \u03b1_c values with dataset-cluster bootstrap 95% CIs excluding 0;\n2. On latent-free data, CAMUV's latent-FP rate tracks \u03b1_c (approximately proportional, checked via slope of FP vs. \u03b1_c), while CAM's E1 rate is large and flat in \u03b1_c;\n3. The \u03b1_c maximizing \u0394 is interior to the grid (a grid-edge optimum downgrades the claim to \"sensitive to threshold\").\nThe primary-cell headline uses \u03b1_c=0.02 fixed in advance; the full grid is reported regardless (no best-\u03b1 cherry-picking).\n\n## 10. Feasibility (projections, explicitly not measurements)\n\nCost model (numpy-class provisioned environment): stage 1 \u2248 O(p\u00b2\u00b7n\u00b7K\u00b2) small; stage 2 dominates \u2248 (#candidate edges \u2248 3p) \u00d7 3 tests \u00d7 B\u00b7m\u00b2 permutation flops. Formula-based projections (arithmetic checked; effective throughput assumed 2\u201310 GFLOP/s/core \u2014 the dominant uncertainty):\n- p=5, n=500: ~5\u201330 s CAMUV per seed; p=10, n=1000: ~1\u20134 min per seed; p=20, n=2000 (with m=1000 subsample, B=200): ~20\u201360 min per seed.\n- Grid: 27 primary cells \u00d7 50 seeds = 1,350 runs + 12 ablation cells \u00d7 20 seeds = 240 runs; projected total \u2248 300\u2013500 CAMUV core-hours plus \u2248 40\u201360 % of that for the CAM baselines (CAM has no permutation stage) \u2192 \u2248 450\u2013800 core-hours; at 224 cores with embarrassingly parallel (cell, seed, method) jobs and thread cap 2/job, wall-clock projection is hours-to-~1 day, dominated by the p=20 tier.\n- **Parallelization**: outer pool over (cell, seed, method) via multiprocessing/joblib across \u2264112 workers \u00d7 2 threads; per-seed independence means no synchronization beyond result-file aggregation.\n- **Stdlib-only pilot scope** (this sandbox, verified package-free): pure-Python HSIC at p=4\u20135, n=200\u2013300, m=150, B=100\u2013200, \u226410 seeds, single cell. It can test: pipeline correctness end-to-end, and whether the latent-confounding signature (both-direction rejection + joint-fit dependence for confounded pairs, single-direction rejection for directed pairs) separates the two edge classes. It cannot support: the full grid, stable AUROC estimates, FCI/RCD baselines, or any threshold-sweep conclusions.\n- **Go/no-go for the full external run**: proceed only if the pilot shows (i) confounded-pair both-rejection rate \u2265 80 % vs. \u2264 30 % for confounder-free pairs at \u03b1=0.05, (ii) latent-free FP rate within 2\u00d7\u03b1, (iii) no degenerate fits / crashes across pilot seeds.\n- **Replication risk**: spline/HSIC substitutions vs. the paper's GAM-boosting CAM primitives can shift finite-sample selection behavior and test power; mitigation \u2014 report both K=6/10 and B=200/500 columns, validate CAM component against published CAM behavior on linear-Gaussian data, and label any quantitative divergence from published CAMUV figures as expected under reimplementation.\n\n## 11. Deliverables / analysis plan (prospective)\n\nPer-cell metric tables keyed by the \u00a73 taxonomy; skeleton/2/3-stage attribution deltas against oracle variants; AUROC/PR curves for S_c; E1/E2/E3 confusion breakdown; sensitivity heatmaps with the \u00a79 criteria evaluated mechanically; pilot go/no-go report; final claims restricted to: skeleton recovery quality, confounding-detection skill, orientation correctness split by confounding status, non-over-invocation on latent-free data, threshold robustness per \u00a79, and runtime scaling.\n\n## 12. Assumptions and unknowns (visible, restated)\n\nAssumptions: reimplementation in a provisioned numpy/scipy/sklearn environment (primary) or stdlib pilot (fallback); \u00a72 DGP restriction (no latent-ancestor pairs) makes G\\* parameter-derived and unambiguous; undirected-prediction scoring rule (strict-1 primary, relaxed-0.5 secondary); CAM primitives approximated by splines.\nUnknowns / unverifiable here: original CAMUV hyperparameters and exact test forms; how published CAMUV scores SHD on marginal graphs; numeric behavior of any stage (nothing has been run); whether CAM-shared-skel or CAM-native widens the gap; absolute runtime until measured. FCI/RCD/lvLiNGAM availability depends on external provisioning.", "evidence": "Inspection (verified): /workspace contains only task.json (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); no CAMUV/causal code or datasets on disk; Python 3.14.4 with numpy/scipy/sklearn/pandas/networkx/rpy2/pgmpy/causal-learn/torch all MISSING; pip present but install fails (no network, missing CA bundle); no R/Rscript/conda/uv; stdlib (random/statistics/multiprocessing) available; 224 cores, ~2 TB RAM, no GPU. Feasibility arithmetic: evaluated the cost model O(p\u00b2\u00b7n\u00b7K\u00b2) + O(3p\u00b73\u00b7B\u00b7m\u00b2) in python for p\u2208{5,10,20}, n\u2208{500,1000,2000} with K=8, B=500 (cost-proxy outputs 5.6 / 45 / 360 GFLOP-units per seed at the three tiers), and counted the grid (27 primary cells \u00d7 50 seeds + 12 ablation cells \u00d7 20 seeds = 1,590 runs) \u2014 these are formula-based projections, not measurements. Repository observation supporting \u00a74/\u00a712 deviations: no CAMUV source exists locally, so spline/HSIC primitives are declared substitutes for the published GAM-boosting CAM construction.", "assumptions": ["Full design assumes a provisioned environment with numpy/scipy/sklearn; the sandbox itself can only run the reduced stdlib pilot.", "\u00a72 DGP restriction (no ancestor-chain within a latent's children; no latent-to-latent or latent-to-parent edges) makes the observed marginal graph equal the parameter-derived target object \u2014 full PAG closure is deliberately out of scope.", "Scoring rules fixed in advance: bidirected mark takes precedence over confounded+direct pairs (subtype recorded); undirected predicted edges score 1 in primary SHD, 0.5 in the pre-specified relaxed variant.", "CAM/GAM primitives approximated by natural-cubic-spline greedy regression with BIC screening; HSIC median-heuristic permutation tests (B=500, m\u22641000) approximate the paper's residual tests.", "Projected costs assume 2-10 GFLOP/s effective per core in the provisioned environment; all runtime numbers are projections, none measured."], "uncertainties": ["Exact published CAMUV test statistics, selection penalties, and hyperparameters remain unverified (source unavailable); quantitative divergence from published results is a declared replication risk.", "Finite-sample separation of the T_fwd/T_bwd/T_na confounding signature (the pilot's go/no-go thresholds) is a hypothesis, not an established fact.", "Whether CAM-shared-skel vs CAM-native ordering of the CAMUV advantage changes the headline conclusion; both are pre-specified.", "FCI/RCD/lvLiNGAM baselines depend entirely on external package provisioning and are optional.", "Absolute runtime at the p=20 tier (20-60 min/seed projection) has an order-of-magnitude uncertainty band until measured."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b047aa37-0253-4849-b9ae-3a4cc3ba58a4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design (prospective, design-only): Evaluating CAMUV for Causal Discovery with Unobserved Common Causes\n\nStatus: no experiment has been run; every quantity below is specified prospectively. Environment facts (no packages, no R, no network; 224 cores, ~2 TB RAM) were verified by inspection; everything else is design.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Question and design logic\n\nQuestion: how can causal relationships be discovered in data with unobserved common causes using CAMUV? The design answers this by (i) constructing additive-noise DGPs where latents enter as shared additive functions, (ii) instantiating CAMUV's three stages (skeleton via sparse non-additive-nonlinear regression \u2192 confounded-vs-orientable classification \u2192 orientation on the remainder), (iii) scoring against a pinned marginal-graph target with a stage-attributable error taxonomy, and (iv) pre-registered threshold-sensitivity analysis showing any CAMUV-over-CAM advantage is not a tuning artifact.\n\n## 2. Data-generating process (fixed specification)\n\n- Observed nodes p \u2208 {5, 10, 20}; latents d \u2208 {1, 2, 3}.\n- Observed DAG: Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi over a random topological order, edge probability set for mean in-degree \u2248 2. Latent children sets: 2\u20134 observed nodes each, sampled so that (DGP restriction, stated as a design choice) latents have no edges to latents and no latent's child set contains an ancestor-chain pair (i.e., no child of L is an ancestor of another child of L). This keeps the ADMG marginal equal to the parameter-derived target object in \u00a73 and avoids PAG closure subtleties.\n- Mechanism: X_j = \u03a3_{k\u2208pa(j)} f_jk(X_k) + \u03a3_{\u2113: j\u2208ch(L_\u2113)} g_j\u2113(L_\u2113) + \u03b5_j, with L_\u2113 ~ N(0,1), \u03b5_j ~ N(0, \u03c3\u00b2). Primary function family: GP draws, RBF kernel, length-scale 1.0, mean-centered; every node standardized to unit variance after generation. Noise: \u03c3 chosen so Var(\u03b5)/Var(signal) = 0.5.\n- Non-additive latent ablation only: (a) multiplicative latent\u2013parent interaction g(X\u00b7L), (b) latent-modulated noise variance. These violate the additive-latent assumption and are diagnostics, not core cells.\n- Nonlinearity knob \u03bb \u2208 {0, 0.5, 1.0}: f \u2190 \u03bb\u00b7f + (1\u2212\u03bb)\u00b7(linear projection of f); \u03bb=1 is primary.\n- Sample sizes n \u2208 {500, 1000, 2000}. Seeds: 50 per primary cell, 20 per ablation cell, seeds fixed 1\u2026S; all methods see the identical dataset per (cell, seed).\n\n## 3. Pinned scoring convention (resolves open mapping choice)\n\n**Primary target object G\\***: a directed/bidirected mixed graph on observed nodes, derived from DGP parameters:\n- pair (i,j) is **adjacent** in G\\* iff a direct edge exists between them OR they share \u22651 latent parent (this adjacency set *is* the Markov closure of latent marginalization under the \u00a72 restriction; no other implied edges arise);\n- if (i,j) shares a latent \u2192 **bidirected** in G\\*, with recorded subtype \"confounded-only\" (no direct edge) or \"confounded+direct\" (direct edge present; the bidirected mark takes precedence for scoring, subtype tracked separately);\n- else, if direct edge i\u2192j \u2192 **directed** i\u2192j; else no edge.\n\nPredicted output object: same vocabulary {none, \u2192, \u2190, \u2194} (CAMUV's PAG-like output; undirected predictions are mapped to a canonical choice per the rule below).\n\n**Per-pair decision rule and SHD** (SHD = sum of per-pair mismatches):\n\n| Truth \\ Prediction | none | correct dir | reversed dir | bidirected \u2194 | undirected \u2014 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| directed i\u2192j | 1 (E4-miss) | 0 | 1 (E3) | 1 (**E2: latent over-invocation**) | 1* |\n| bidirected | 1 (E4-miss) | 1 (**E1: wrongly oriented despite latent**) | 1 (**E1**) | 0 | 1* |\n| none | 0 | 1 (E4-spurious) | 1 (E4-spurious) | 1 (E4-spurious) | 1 |\n\n*Undirected predictions enter SHD as 1 in the primary metric (strict rule); a secondary \"relaxed SHD\" counting them as 0.5 is reported only as a sensitivity column, pre-specified here.\n\n**Skeleton adjacency P/R/F1 and AUPRC**: predicted skeleton (any non-none edge) vs. G\\*-skeleton; AUPRC uses stage-1 continuous edge scores when available.\n\n**Orientation accuracy, split by construction**:\n- **Orientation recall on confounder-free edges** = (# true directed, non-confounded edges predicted with the exact orientation) / (# such true edges); missing or bidirected predictions count against the numerator.\n- E1 rate (reversed-should-be-bidirected) reported separately from E2 (over-invocation) and E3 (plain reversal), giving a full confusion breakdown per cell \u2014 this is the core evidence that CAMUV's latent handling changes the error mix.\n\n**Latent-confounded-pair detection**: positives = pairs sharing a latent; score = stage-2 confounding score (Section 4, S_c); AUROC + average precision over all pairs (primary) and over candidate edges only (secondary).\n\n## 4. CAMUV pipeline, operationalized (with explicit deviations)\n\n**Stage 1 \u2014 skeleton (candidate edges).** For each target j, greedy forward addition over predictors k \u2260 j using additive cubic-spline regression (K=8 natural cubic spline basis functions per predictor, BIC/screening p-value stop), **plus** pairwise tensor-product interaction bases (4\u00d74 truncated) tested as a group for already-selected predictor pairs (this is the non-additive-nonlinear (NAN) component). Candidate skeleton = union of selected (target, predictor) pairs, symmetrized, across all j. Record continuous selection strength per edge for AUPRC.\n\n**Stage 2 \u2014 confounded vs. orientable.** For each candidate edge (i,j), with N\u0302(\u00b7) the stage-1 neighborhoods, three tests (Gaussian-kernel HSIC, median heuristic bandwidth, permutation p-values, B=500; subsample m=min(n,1000) when n>1000):\n- **T_fwd**: ANM residual-independence in direction N\u0302(j)\u2192j \u2014 HSIC between residual of additive fit of X_j on N\u0302(j)\\{i} and the fitted predictor block (equivalently residual vs. the excluded predictor X_i, the CAM-style check). Tests whether j's additive model violates noise/predictor independence when i is excluded from its parents.\n- **T_bwd**: symmetric, N\u0302(i)\u2192i.\n- **T_na (joint non-additivity/residual test)**: fit the joint additive model of the pair \u2014 X_i on N\u0302(i)\\{j} plus f(j) additive term, X_j on N\u0302(j)\\{i} plus additive term from i; test independence of the two pair-residuals (r_i, r_j) by HSIC, and separately an F-test on the group of (i,j) tensor-interaction basis functions added to the joint fit. The confounding signature is that the pair's mutual dependence cannot be absorbed additively in either direction.\n- **Decision rule (Holm-corrected over the 3 tests at \u03b1_c)**: mark i\u2194j (bidirected) iff T_fwd rejected **and** T_bwd rejected **and** T_na/T_add residual dependence rejected. Exactly one of T_fwd/T_bwd rejected \u2192 not confounded; orientable in the surviving direction. Both rejected but joint-additive residuals independent (dependence absorbable additively) \u2192 **kept as orientable/undirected, not bidirected** \u2014 this implements the required principle that ambiguous orientation alone is not evidence of confounding; the joint-fit test is necessary evidence.\n- Confounding score S_c(i,j) = \u2212log10(min p-value across the three-stage gate) for AUROC.\n\n**Stage 3 \u2014 orientation.** CAM-style greedy parent-set selection with significance stop, then topological-order search / edge pruning, restricted to non-confounded candidate edges; bidirected marks from stage 2 are fixed and never oriented.\n\n**Declared deviations from published CAMUV** (source unavailable: R-only, no R/rpy2/network here): (i) spline-basis greedy fits replace GAM-boosting CAM primitives; (ii) our T_fwd/T_bwd/T_na construction emulates their NAN-regression + latent-detection statistics rather than replicating their exact test forms and covariance-penalty selection; (iii) hyperparameters (K, B, m) are ours, not the paper's. All three are flagged in the final report as replication risk.\n\n## 5. Stage-attributable evaluation (resolves interpretability)\n\nPer cell, report the decomposition:\n- **(a) Stage 1 only**: skeleton P/R/F1/AUPRC vs. G\\*-skeleton.\n- **(b) Stage 2 in isolation**: AUROC/AP of S_c for confounded-pair detection, restricted to true-skeleton pairs (primary) and all pairs (secondary).\n- **(c) Stage 3 conditional**: orientation accuracy computed (i) over cells/seeds where stage 1 recovered the skeleton exactly, and (ii) over edges whose stage-2 confounding label was correct \u2014 isolating pure orientation skill.\n\n**Diagnostic variants** (run on the primary grid):\n- **CAMUV-oracle-skel**: true G\\*-skeleton fed to stage 2 (isolates stage-1 error's contribution).\n- **CAMUV-oracle-uv**: true confounded-pair set given; stage 3 only (isolates orientation).\n- Attribution table: \u0394(SHD) between end-to-end and each oracle variant; requirement for the interpretability claim is that the sum of stage deltas tracks end-to-end SHD within a documented tolerance.\n\n## 6. Baselines and fairness (resolved)\n\n- **Core required**: (1) CAMUV (ours, full pipeline); (2) **CAM-native** (B\u00fchlmann-style CAM end-to-end, its own greedy selection); (3) **CAM-shared-skel** \u2014 CAM forced to search only within CAMUV's stage-1 candidate skeleton, so orientation differences cannot be attributed to skeleton differences; (4) latent-free control condition (\u00a77). **Primary comparison for the orientation claim is CAM-shared-skel vs. CAMUV** (identical candidate edges); the native-vs-full comparison is reported secondarily.\n- **External-provisioning-dependent optional baselines** (not core requirements; each carries reimplementation/install risk since nothing is installed here): FCI (causal-learn, kernel or rank-Fisher CI test), RCD, and lvLiNGAM restricted to the \u03bb=0 linear ablation. Each is labeled as such in the report.\n\n## 7. Controls\n\n- **Latent-free condition** (d=0, same DAG/mechanism/noise generators and seeds): requirement \u2014 CAMUV's confounded-pair false-positive rate \u2248 \u03b1_c (within a pre-specified factor 2) and output contains no bidirected edges beyond that rate; CAM on the same data is the sanity oracle. This tests non-over-invocation of latents.\n- Shared datasets/seeds across all methods; identical preprocessing (standardization) across methods.\n\n## 8. Ablations\n\nd \u2208 {0,1,2,3}; latent strength (latent share of child variance: 0.2/0.5/0.8); additive vs. non-additive latent entry (multiplicative interaction; latent-heteroscedastic); \u03bb \u2208 {0, 0.5, 1.0}; n \u2208 {500,1000,2000}; p \u2208 {5,10,20}; function family (GP-RBF vs. random cubic splines vs. small random MLPs).\n\n## 9. Pre-registered threshold-sensitivity analysis (resolves artifact concern)\n\nSweeps (paired over identical datasets and seeds): \u03b1_c \u2208 {0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.1} for stage-2 tests; \u03b1_stop \u2208 {0.001, 0.01, 0.05} for CAM/orientation; stage-1 penalty multiplier \u03c1 \u2208 {1,2,3}\u00d7BIC-equivalent. B, m, K fixed except in the explicit K/B sensitivity column.\n\nPre-registered robustness criteria for the claim \"CAMUV's advantage over CAM is not a tuned-threshold artifact\":\n1. Paired \u0394 metrics (SHD, orientation recall on confounder-free edges, E1 rate) between CAMUV and CAM-shared-skel have consistent sign at \u22654 of 5 \u03b1_c values with dataset-cluster bootstrap 95% CIs excluding 0;\n2. On latent-free data, CAMUV's latent-FP rate tracks \u03b1_c (approximately proportional, checked via slope of FP vs. \u03b1_c), while CAM's E1 rate is large and flat in \u03b1_c;\n3. The \u03b1_c maximizing \u0394 is interior to the grid (a grid-edge optimum downgrades the claim to \"sensitive to threshold\").\nThe primary-cell headline uses \u03b1_c=0.02 fixed in advance; the full grid is reported regardless (no best-\u03b1 cherry-picking).\n\n## 10. Feasibility (projections, explicitly not measurements)\n\nCost model (numpy-class provisioned environment): stage 1 \u2248 O(p\u00b2\u00b7n\u00b7K\u00b2) small; stage 2 dominates \u2248 (#candidate edges \u2248 3p) \u00d7 3 tests \u00d7 B\u00b7m\u00b2 permutation flops. Formula-based projections (arithmetic checked; effective throughput assumed 2\u201310 GFLOP/s/core \u2014 the dominant uncertainty):\n- p=5, n=500: ~5\u201330 s CAMUV per seed; p=10, n=1000: ~1\u20134 min per seed; p=20, n=2000 (with m=1000 subsample, B=200): ~20\u201360 min per seed.\n- Grid: 27 primary cells \u00d7 50 seeds = 1,350 runs + 12 ablation cells \u00d7 20 seeds = 240 runs; projected total \u2248 300\u2013500 CAMUV core-hours plus \u2248 40\u201360 % of that for the CAM baselines (CAM has no permutation stage) \u2192 \u2248 450\u2013800 core-hours; at 224 cores with embarrassingly parallel (cell, seed, method) jobs and thread cap 2/job, wall-clock projection is hours-to-~1 day, dominated by the p=20 tier.\n- **Parallelization**: outer pool over (cell, seed, method) via multiprocessing/joblib across \u2264112 workers \u00d7 2 threads; per-seed independence means no synchronization beyond result-file aggregation.\n- **Stdlib-only pilot scope** (this sandbox, verified package-free): pure-Python HSIC at p=4\u20135, n=200\u2013300, m=150, B=100\u2013200, \u226410 seeds, single cell. It can test: pipeline correctness end-to-end, and whether the latent-confounding signature (both-direction rejection + joint-fit dependence for confounded pairs, single-direction rejection for directed pairs) separates the two edge classes. It cannot support: the full grid, stable AUROC estimates, FCI/RCD baselines, or any threshold-sweep conclusions.\n- **Go/no-go for the full external run**: proceed only if the pilot shows (i) confounded-pair both-rejection rate \u2265 80 % vs. \u2264 30 % for confounder-free pairs at \u03